  "summary": "Pinstripes, jelly buttons and brushed metal: the OS that made computers pretty.",
  "description": "Aqua — Mac OS X's interface from 2000 — established the 2000s' visual standard for software: candy-gloss gel buttons with real specular physics, pinstriped window backgrounds, brushed-metal panels, drop shadows like print, and motion (the genie effect, dock magnification) that made computing feel material. Its eras: early pinstripe/aqua (10.0-10.2), brushed metal decade (10.3-10.9) with iTunes' and Safari's metal chrome, before Yosemite (2014) flattened everything. Aqua's influence is total — every \"modern OS\" of the 2000s (Vista glass, KDE Oxygen) answered it — and its buttons remain the most-imitated object in UI history.",
  "culturalContext": "Aqua defined 2000s software beauty: gel buttons and brushed metal set the standard every OS answered, its polish elevated interface design to brand asset, and Yosemite's flattening closed the era — leaving Aqua buttons the most-imitated objects in UI history.",
